Preventive Note
Incident: Floor 6 Legal Slow/Failed Logon
Date: 2026-08-14

Issue
- Users on Floor 6 Legal experienced slow or failed Windows sign-in at the start of the business day.
- The incident occurred after a recent Windows 11 and Intune migration/change window.
- Service impact was high because affected users could not begin work on time.
- Incident has been resolved and login was verified as successful after remediation.

Fix
- Confirmed the issue was floor-wide rather than isolated to one user or one device.
- Reviewed recent sign-in/profile-related configuration changes for the affected cohort.
- Paused or rolled back the suspected policy/configuration path for the impacted scope.
- Re-synced affected devices to apply corrected effective configuration.
- Re-tested sign-in with affected and control users.
- Verified successful login and no immediate repeat reports.

Cause
- Most probable cause from RCA: a shared sign-in policy/profile-state drift or misapplied configuration in the Win11 + Intune migrated baseline.
- Why this is credible:
  - Multiple users on one floor were affected at the same time.
  - Timing aligned with post-change period.
  - Service recovered after change containment and policy re-sync actions.
- Remaining precision gap: exact misconfigured setting/value and change artifact are still to confirm from policy diff and change logs.

Preventive note (detailed)

1) Change gating and rollout safety
- Introduce mandatory staged rollout for sign-in/profile/identity-affecting policy changes.
- Start with a pilot group, then a limited business cohort, then full scope only after successful checks.
- Block production-wide deployment if pilot sign-in checks fail or are incomplete.
- Require an explicit go/no-go decision record before each expansion stage.

2) Pre-deployment validation checklist (must pass before rollout)
- Confirm policy intent, target scope, and exclusion scope are peer-reviewed.
- Confirm assignment does not unintentionally include high-impact floors.
- Validate baseline compatibility against migrated Windows 11 cohort.
- Validate rollback path and rollback owner are defined before deployment.
- Validate service desk readiness: known symptoms, first triage actions, and escalation path.

3) Post-deployment early-warning controls
- Enforce a monitoring window immediately after each rollout phase.
- Watch for sign-in failures, sign-in delay complaints, and profile-load error patterns.
- Require checkpoint sign-offs at fixed intervals (for example, 30/60/120 minutes after release) before widening scope.
- If thresholds are exceeded, auto-trigger containment: pause rollout and revert assignment scope.

4) Rollback readiness standard
- Keep a documented pre-change snapshot of policy assignment and critical setting values.
- Store one-click rollback steps in the change record, including exact target groups.
- Define an on-call escalation chain for failed rollback.
- Test rollback procedure quarterly in a non-production or controlled test scope.

5) Evidence and incident response standardization
- Use a standard incident evidence pack for login incidents:
  - affected user/device pair,
  - sign-in timing before/after,
  - security/profile log extracts,
  - policy assignment snapshot,
  - sync timestamps,
  - verification outcome.
- Require this pack before closure to improve repeatability and audit quality.

6) Ownership and accountability model
- Service desk owner:
  - maintain symptom playbook,
  - ensure ticket evidence quality,
  - execute initial scope checks.
- Endpoint/Intune owner:
  - approve and execute scoped containment,
  - validate assignment correctness,
  - complete policy-diff analysis.
- Change manager:
  - enforce rollout gates,
  - verify rollback readiness,
  - ensure post-change reviews are completed.

7) Verification rules before declaring stable
- Validate at least one previously affected user can sign in successfully.
- Validate at least one additional user on another affected device can sign in successfully.
- Validate no repeated sign-in/profile failure pattern during defined observation window.
- Validate policy sync success on sampled affected devices.
- Keep incident in monitoring state until all checks pass.

8) Knowledge and communication improvements
- Keep L1, L2, and Runbook documents synchronized after each incident learning.
- Publish plain-language user guidance quickly during active disruption.
- Provide leadership updates that separate resolved items from open risks.
- Train first responders on scope containment to avoid broad rollback errors.

9) Recurrence prevention checkpoints (operational cadence)
- Weekly: review all recent identity/sign-in policy changes and near misses.
- Monthly: audit target group assignments and drift in sign-in/profile baselines.
- Quarterly: tabletop incident drill for floor-wide sign-in outage and rollback.

10) Closure condition for preventive actions
- Preventive actions are complete only when:
  - staged rollout gates are active,
  - threshold-based rollback triggers are defined and tested,
  - standard evidence pack is in use,
  - ownership and review cadence are formally assigned.